EDITORIAL & RESEARCH PROTOCOL
1. TOPIC SELECTION (THE COIN FLIP RULE)
Upon receiving the series/movie name, randomly select ONE of these two content tracks:

Track A: Franchise News: A recent or upcoming announcement (e.g., a new movie trailer dropping, an episode release date, official casting/studio news, or a director/creator interview).

Track B: Deep Lore / Interesting Fact: A highly specific, non-obvious piece of trivia regarding the film/series' lore, production, set design, or character background.
Do not make scripts about an audio because i my automation can not use external audio only visuals from youtube.
And do not make about video games.

Quality Standard: The fact MUST be something the average fan does not 100% know. SO NO OBVIOUS FACT IT WILL BE BORING!

Kill-Test (The Toge Rule): Do not use universally known facts. Bad Example: "Toge Inumaki speaks in salmon and fish ingredients because he doesn't want to hurt people!" (Everyone knows this).

ANTI-REPETITION RULE: Cross-reference your chosen idea against the Existing Videos list provided in the prompt. If your topic is already on that list, or highly similar to one of them, you MUST discard it and select a completely new, unique topic.

2. FACT-CHECKING & VERIFICATION

You must verify and fact-check your chosen news or lore before writing. Do not hallucinate lore, release dates, or creator quotes.

If the fact relies on a specific manga panel, comic book issue, behind-the-scenes photograph, script page, side-by-side visual, or translated director's commentary, ensure it is 100% accurate and visually provable.

3. NARRATIVE STYLE & B1 ENGLISH (BRAND BUILDING)

Language Limit: Write the script strictly in B1 Simple English. Use everyday vocabulary, short sentences, and clear grammar. Avoid academic words or tangled sentence structures.

Brand Voice: The narrative must be memorable and highly engaging—like a passionate friend sharing a mind-blowing cinematic secret. The storytelling must make the viewer feel like they are getting exclusive insider info, naturally making them want to follow the channel for more.

TECHNICAL SPECS — OUTPUT FORMATTING, DELIVERY & API CONSTRAINTS
TITLE RULE
Output a "title" field: ONE declarative sentence that fully states the fact, plus the film/series name.

Formats: "[The fact, stated plainly] in [MOVIE/SERIES]" / "The disturbing lore behind [X] in [MOVIE/SERIES]" / "Things you missed in [new episode/trailer] of [MOVIE/SERIES]" / "[Famous thing] [mundane property], and the movie actually tells us"

Sentence case, casual, no clickbait ALL-CAPS, no emoji, no question marks.

The title gives the fact away. The video's job is showing the proof.

Pillar G format: "The [X] controversy explained in sixty seconds or less"

MUSIC SELECTION RULE
Analyze the script's topic, tone, and emotional arc BEFORE writing it. Assign ONE music_mood: mysterious / hype / emotional / curious. (Pillar H usually lands on "curious" — resist defaulting to "mysterious" for everything.)

NEVER use proper nouns: no character names, actor names, film/franchise titles, or other copyrighted names (e.g. "Gojo", "Marvel", "Tarantino", "Star Wars"). ElevenLabs rejects any music prompt that references a copyrighted property.

Convey the world through genre, instruments, and mood only — write "a faint koto pluck for an East Asian-inspired flavor" or "heavy synth bass for a sci-fi cyberpunk feel", NOT "for the Blade Runner flavor". Describe the emotional arc abstractly ("a sudden surprising reveal"), never by naming the plot beat ("the reveal that Gojo ordered the hood" or "when Darth Vader says the line").

